---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/firepush';
const title = 'Firepush for Shopify: evaluate the browser subscription';
const desc = 'Assess current Firepush web push scope, browser eligibility, cart cancellation, stock alerts and notification workload.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Shopify operations · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Firepush for Shopify: evaluate the browser subscription</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Firepush is now a web push candidate, with a different reach and identity model from email or SMS. Evaluate it for shoppers who can subscribe in a supported browser and return to a useful product or cart link. Its potential benefit is an additional opted-in notification route; traffic share alone does not establish reachable subscribers, cheap acquisition or incremental recovery.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Current scope takes precedence over historical SMS copy</h2>
      <p>Firepush’s <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/blog/targeted-email-marketing/">email article, updated November 18, 2026</a>, says it no longer offers SMS or email campaigns. That is an article update date, not a verified shutdown date. Older <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/blog/sms-campaigns/">SMS articles</a> remain discoverable; they are not a basis for recommending a new Firepush SMS sequence. This review therefore evaluates web push rather than a three-channel bundle.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://getfirepush.com/">current supplier site</a> lists welcome, abandoned-cart, price-drop, back-in-stock and order notifications. It describes purchase cancellation of cart reminders, browser support and an iPhone home-screen installation requirement. Treat those as capabilities to verify in the store, not measured delivery speed or proof that every visitor is reachable. Plan allowances and limits should be confirmed in the current dashboard before launch.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Browser ownership is the central integration question</h2>
      <p>Create a test matrix by browser, operating system and installation state. A large desktop share may justify testing, but the denominator should be eligible visitors who see the prompt, then subscriptions, successful sends and clicks. On iPhone, test the home-screen route separately from ordinary browsing. A browser permission prompt is a distinct action from joining an email list or entering a phone number at checkout.</p>
      <p>Identify what links a browser subscription to its cart and later order. Ask the operator to demonstrate purchase suppression on the same browser, a second device and a guest checkout. A shopper may have more than one subscription; an email match does not automatically prove device ownership. Record unlinked cases instead of merging tokens by assumption. Check permission revocation and expired subscriptions, and distinguish accepted delivery from a notification actually seen.</p>
      <p>Assign the storefront team the prompt and installation experience, the ecommerce team inventory and order truth, and a named retention owner the send rules. Set a reviewable promotional budget per subscription based on response and withdrawals. Cart, restock and promotional alerts can collide; one blanket two-per-week rule cannot resolve every product-specific waitlist or service event.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Four proposed acceptance exercises</h2>
      <p>None of these exercises has been run on merchant accounts. The timings, audience sizes and prices below are hypothetical inputs. Preserve event IDs and expected outcomes so failures can be reproduced rather than presented as conversion results.</p>
      <p><strong>Electronics comparison cart:</strong> start with a $130 cart and a candidate 30-minute delay. Use an opted-in supported browser, then buy during the wait. Confirm cancellation, restore a cart after a product becomes unavailable and replay the cart update. Check destination links and currency after session expiry. Compare email alone with email plus push only among people eligible for both; otherwise channel eligibility confounds the result.</p>
      <p><strong>Limited restock:</strong> propose a waitlist of 400 subscriptions for one variant. Confirm whether the store can capture and target that exact variant before promising a native waitlist. Replenish a small quantity, purchase it down and inspect availability at click. Decide whether stock reservation exists; a notification must not imply a reserved item. Separate requested restock alerts from general promotional permission.</p>
      <p><strong>Fashion recovery handoff:</strong> replace the obsolete Firepush SMS proposal with a push-versus-existing-recovery exercise. List the current email or SMS sender, each step and its cancellation signal. Verify a purchase reaches both queues and suppress duplicate incentives. A store with 280 monthly orders is a possible workload, not a threshold for adding SMS or moving vendors. Keep any existing SMS work with a currently supported service only if the store needs it.</p>
      <p><strong>Home-goods price drop:</strong> use a lamp reduced from $240 by 15%, giving $204 before shipping or tax. Establish whether the eligible relationship is a followed product, explicit alert request or tracked view; do not treat every historical viewer as a subscriber. A proposed 14-day interest window is an audience rule. Purchase, revocation and variant unavailability should prevent a stale or irrelevant alert.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Measure reach, scarcity and contribution separately</h2>
      <p>For cart recovery, randomize at a stable subscription or reconciled-person level and keep assignment consistent across repeated carts. Report eligible subscriptions, sends, clicks, orders and net contribution after discounts, returns and fees. The old claim of 4.2% incremental recovery has no demonstrated experiment here and should not be used for budgeting. Multiple devices can inflate both audience size and attribution unless reconciled explicitly.</p>
      <p>For restocks, log inventory at send and click, stockouts and the share of subscribers who had a chance to buy. A scarce launch can sell out without additional notifications; sales attributed to push do not alone show incremental demand. Where a holdout would change access to scarce inventory, report that experimental limitation and use the exercise to validate delivery and relevance rather than claim causal lift.</p>
      <p>For price drops, evaluate contribution on the reduced selling price and include purchases that would have occurred anyway. Track notification withdrawal and invalid subscriptions alongside short-term clicks. Diagnose changes by browser cohort; a loss of eligible subscriptions is different from weak creative among still-reachable shoppers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Cost the send workload and decide whether to keep push</h2>
      <p>Quote push notifications per month, not orders or assumed SMS credits. For example, 2,000 reachable subscriptions receiving four campaigns generate 8,000 attempted campaign notifications before welcome, cart, restock and order messages. Ask how attempts, failures and retries count, what happens at the cap, and whether automation stops with campaigns. Do not promise the same reach from 2,000 visitors or customer profiles.</p>
      <p>Compare dated currency, billing term, allowance, cap behavior and support with the same workload elsewhere. Include prompt setup, browser testing and ongoing inventory/queue reconciliation. There is no verified $12 startup stack, $35 retained plan or universal order-volume graduation path in this evaluation. An email or SMS supplier is not compulsory simply because push is installed.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="/apps/drip">Drip review</a> evaluates person-level events and maintained segment logic; this page evaluates permission and routing tied to browser subscriptions. The <a class="link" href="/apps/activecampaign">ActiveCampaign review</a> instead follows rep-owned quotes. Choose the record model that matches the job. If considering <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> for consolidation, prove browser coverage, permission migration and cancellation rather than assuming tokens transfer between suppliers.</p>
      <p>Before changing push providers, inventory subscription ownership, export capability, portability and any need to ask shoppers to subscribe again. Retain Firepush only if its reachable audience, useful alerts and maintenance cost justify it. The decision should follow observed store evidence, not a fabricated merchant migration or supplier ranking.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
